/* lore/en.lore.js — descriptions for the "Описание созвездий" tab (core/lore.js). v618 (owner): written in English first,
   from English-language sources (official books as summarised by the English wikis), in our own words; the other six
   languages are translated from the English. Loaded only when the tab is opened. */
KC.addLore("en", {
  "dnd.c.artificer": "The Artificer is an inventor who treats magic as a craft, channelling spells through tools and gadgets rather than prayer or old tomes. Artificers infuse ordinary items with magical properties and can repair, improve and improvise devices on the fly. The class is rooted in the world of Eberron and is also presented in Tasha's Cauldron of Everything.",
  "dnd.c.barbarian": "The Barbarian is a fierce warrior driven by primal fury. In battle they enter a Rage that makes their blows land harder and lets them shrug off physical harm, and they fight best in light armour or none, relying on toughness and instinct. Many come from the wild fringes of civilisation, though any deep wellspring of anger can fuel the gift.",
  "dnd.c.bard": "The Bard works magic through music, speech and performance, in a world said to have been spoken into being. Bards bolster allies with Bardic Inspiration, know a little about almost everything, and command a broad range of spells for charm, healing and trickery. They gather in loose colleges that share stories and techniques.",
  "dnd.c.cleric": "The Cleric is a priestly champion who wields divine magic granted by a god or a higher power. Clerics heal and protect their companions, turn back the undead and call down their deity's might through Channel Divinity. Each serves a divine domain that shapes the spells and gifts they receive.",
  "dnd.c.druid": "The Druid is a guardian of the natural world who draws power from nature itself or from nature deities. Druids take the shapes of animals through Wild Shape and cast spells that call storms, mend wounds and command plants and beasts. They gather in circles that share a particular view of nature's power.",
  "dnd.c.fighter": "The Fighter is a master of weapons and armour, trained to excel in every kind of combat. Fighters make extra attacks, push past their limits with Action Surge and catch their breath with Second Wind. Soldiers, mercenaries, knights and duellists all fit within the class.",
  "dnd.c.monk": "The Monk is a martial artist who channels inner energy, called ki (Focus in the 2024 rules), into extraordinary feats. Monks strike with fists and simple weapons at blinding speed, deflect missiles and move faster than ordinary people. Their training comes from monasteries that follow distinct traditions.",
  "dnd.c.paladin": "The Paladin is a holy warrior bound by a sacred oath that is the true source of their power. Paladins combine armoured combat with divine magic, healing with Lay on Hands and pouring radiant energy into their blows with Divine Smite. Their oath, rather than any temple, defines the ideals they fight for.",
  "dnd.c.ranger": "The Ranger is a hunter and scout of the wilderness who blends martial skill with nature magic. Rangers excel at tracking, surviving in the wild and fighting with bow or blade, and they often study particular foes and terrains. They guard the borders between civilisation and the dangers beyond it.",
  "dnd.c.rogue": "The Rogue relies on skill, stealth and precision rather than brute strength. Rogues deal a devastating Sneak Attack when they catch a foe off guard, slip away with Cunning Action and master a wide range of skills, from picking locks to talking their way out of trouble. Thieves, spies, scouts and assassins all fall under the class.",
  "dnd.c.sorcerer": "The Sorcerer carries magic in their very being, often through a bloodline or an extraordinary event. Instead of studying spells, sorcerers channel innate power and shape it with Metamagic, spending sorcery points to twist, extend or quicken their spells. The origin of their power determines their special gifts.",
  "dnd.c.warlock": "The Warlock gains magic through a pact with a powerful otherworldly patron. Warlocks have few spell slots that return after a short rest, signature cantrips such as Eldritch Blast, and Eldritch Invocations that grant unusual abilities. The nature of the patron colours both their powers and their personality.",
  "dnd.c.wizard": "The Wizard is a scholarly spellcaster who masters magic through study and records spells in a spellbook. With the broadest spell list of any class, wizards can prepare the right spell for nearly any situation and regain some spent magic through Arcane Recovery. Many specialise in a single school of magic.",
  "dnd.s.artificer.alchemist": "The Alchemist is an Artificer who specialises in mixing potent concoctions. They brew experimental elixirs with unpredictable helpful effects, such as healing, flight or resilience, and use alchemical know-how to strengthen their healing and their acid and fire spells. Later they can cure ailments and restore great amounts of vitality.",
  "dnd.s.artificer.armorer": "The Armorer is an Artificer who turns a suit of armour into a magical second skin. Their Arcane Armor can be set to the Guardian model, with thunder gauntlets and a protective field, or the Infiltrator model, with a lightning launcher and enhanced stealth. As they advance, the armour holds more infusions and grows more powerful.",
  "dnd.s.artificer.artillerist": "The Artillerist is an Artificer who specialises in explosive, destructive magic. Their signature creation is the Eldritch Cannon, a small magical turret that can blast enemies with fire, hurl them back with force or shield allies. They also channel spells through an arcane firearm for extra damage.",
  "dnd.s.artificer.battlesmith": "The Battle Smith is an Artificer who blends battlefield prowess with repair and protection. They build a Steel Defender, a loyal mechanical companion that fights at their side, and can use their Intelligence to attack with magic weapons. Their magic leans toward healing and shielding allies.",
  "dnd.s.barbarian.beast": "The Path of the Beast is a Barbarian path whose rage awakens a bestial nature within. While raging, the barbarian grows natural weapons such as a bite, claws or a tail, later gains animal-like adaptations for climbing, swimming and leaping, and can spread a maddening fury to foes. The beast's source may be a curse, an ancestry or a primal spirit.",
  "dnd.s.barbarian.berserker": "The Path of the Berserker is a Barbarian path for those who lose themselves in pure violence. Its signature Frenzy lets the barbarian strike extra blows while raging, and later they resist fear and charm while raging and can terrify foes with a glare. It is the most direct expression of rage.",
  "dnd.s.barbarian.stormherald": "The Path of the Storm Herald is a Barbarian path whose rage takes the form of a primal storm. While raging, the barbarian projects an aura shaped by a chosen environment: desert heat, sea lightning or bracing tundra cold that grants temporary hit points. The storm also grants resistances and later lashes out at nearby enemies.",
  "dnd.s.barbarian.wildheart": "The Path of the Wild Heart is a Barbarian path, the 2024 version of the Totem Warrior, whose fury is bound to animal spirits. The barbarian draws on beasts such as the bear, eagle and wolf for resilience, mobility or teamwork, and can commune with animals. Its powers grow from kinship with the natural world.",
  "dnd.s.bard.creation": "The College of Creation is a Bard college inspired by the Song of Creation, the primordial melody said to have shaped the cosmos. These bards empower allies with Notes of Potential and can sing nonmagical objects into existence. At higher levels they can bring an item to life as a dancing animated companion.",
  "dnd.s.bard.dance": "The College of Dance is a Bard college, introduced in the 2024 Player's Handbook, whose members express magic through movement. Their Dazzling Footwork turns graceful motion into defence and unarmed strikes, and they help allies reposition and act quickly in battle. Later they guide companions out of danger with nimble evasion.",
  "dnd.s.bard.eloquence": "The College of Eloquence is a Bard college devoted to persuasive speech and rhetoric. Its bards rarely fail when persuading or deceiving, can sap an enemy's resolve with Unsettling Words, and inspire allies so well that their encouragement is seldom wasted. At higher levels any creature can understand them when they speak.",
  "dnd.s.bard.glamour": "The College of Glamour is a Bard college rooted in the beguiling magic of the Feywild. These bards wrap allies in a Mantle of Inspiration, captivate audiences with enthralling performances, and can take on an unearthly majesty that commands obedience. Their art borrows the grace and allure of the fey.",
  "dnd.s.cleric.life": "The Life Domain is a Cleric domain focused on the positive energy that sustains all living things. Its clerics are the strongest healers, adding extra healing to their spells and restoring many allies at once with Preserve Life. They often serve gods of healing, harvest and vitality.",
  "dnd.s.cleric.tempest": "The Tempest Domain is a Cleric domain belonging to gods of storms, sea and sky. Its clerics wield thunder and lightning, lash back at attackers with storm power, and channel divinity to deal maximum lightning or thunder damage. They often wear heavy armour and fight on the front line.",
  "dnd.s.cleric.trickery": "The Trickery Domain is a Cleric domain serving gods of mischief, thieves and deception. Its clerics bless allies with stealth, create illusory duplicates of themselves with Invoke Duplicity, and favour spells of illusion and misdirection. They act as cunning agents who undermine tyranny and pride.",
  "dnd.s.druid.dreams": "The Circle of Dreams is a Druid circle tied to the Feywild and the realm of dreams. Its druids heal allies with the restorative Balm of the Summer Court, shelter their camp with protective magic, and travel hidden paths that let them teleport short distances. They guard the gentle mysteries of the fey.",
  "dnd.s.druid.land": "The Circle of the Land is a Druid circle of mystics and sages who safeguard ancient knowledge. Each druid draws power from a chosen type of land, gaining spells tied to it, and can recover spent magic while resting. The 2014 rules offer terrains such as arctic, desert, forest and mountain, while the 2024 rules simplify them to arid, polar, temperate and tropical.",
  "dnd.s.druid.moon": "The Circle of the Moon is a Druid circle of fierce guardians who rely on Wild Shape in battle. They can transform quickly into stronger beasts than other druids, sustain themselves while transformed, and eventually take elemental forms. They watch over wild places under the changing moon.",
  "dnd.s.druid.sea": "The Circle of the Sea is a Druid circle, introduced in the 2024 Player's Handbook, that channels the ocean's tides and storms. By spending Wild Shape, these druids surround themselves with Wrath of the Sea, an aura of ocean spray that deals cold damage and pushes enemies away. Later they swim freely, fly on storm winds and resist cold, lightning and thunder.",
  "dnd.s.fighter.battlemaster": "The Battle Master is a Fighter archetype for tacticians who treat combat as an academic discipline. They spend superiority dice on maneuvers such as disarming, tripping or rallying allies, and can size up opponents to learn their strengths. Their skill rests on long-honed technique rather than raw strength.",
  "dnd.s.fighter.champion": "The Champion is a Fighter archetype focused on raw physical excellence. Champions land critical hits more often, gain improved athleticism, and eventually recover hit points each turn when badly hurt. It is the simplest and most direct path of the fighter.",
  "dnd.s.fighter.runeknight": "The Rune Knight is a Fighter archetype that wields the rune magic of giants. They inscribe magical runes on their gear for various effects and can grow to Large size with Giant's Might, dealing extra damage. As they grow stronger, the knight can become even larger and invoke runes more often.",
  "dnd.s.monk.astral": "The Way of the Astral Self is a Monk tradition in which ki takes the shape of the monk's true self projected from the Astral Plane. These monks summon spectral astral arms to strike from farther away, and later a visage and a full astral body that sharpen their senses and defence. Their practice is a journey of self-discovery.",
  "dnd.s.monk.mercy": "The Warrior of Mercy is a Monk subclass, called the Way of Mercy in Tasha's Cauldron of Everything, whose monks manipulate life force to heal or harm. Often wearing a distinctive mask, they can mend wounds and cure ailments with a touch, or inflict extra necrotic damage on foes. They walk the line between life and death.",
  "dnd.s.monk.openhand": "The Warrior of the Open Hand is a Monk subclass, formerly the Way of the Open Hand, for masters of unarmed combat. Their flurries of blows can knock enemies prone, push them away or stop them from reacting, and they can heal themselves through Wholeness of Body. At the peak of their training they learn the deadly Quivering Palm.",
  "dnd.s.paladin.conquest": "The Oath of Conquest is a Paladin oath for those who seek glory through victory and absolute order. Its paladins spread fear on the battlefield and their aura punishes frightened foes, in the belief that strength must crush chaos. Their tenets call for extinguishing all hope in their enemies.",
  "dnd.s.paladin.devotion": "The Oath of Devotion is a Paladin oath that embodies the classic ideals of the shining knight: honesty, courage, compassion and duty. Its paladins make their weapon glow with holy power through Sacred Weapon, and their aura protects allies from being charmed. They strive to be beacons of justice and virtue.",
  "dnd.s.paladin.glory": "The Oath of Glory is a Paladin oath for heroes who believe they and their companions are destined for great deeds. Its paladins excel at athletic feats, inspire allies through their smites, and grant extra speed with their aura. They train relentlessly to live up to the legendary heroes they admire.",
  "dnd.s.paladin.vengeance": "The Oath of Vengeance is a Paladin oath dedicated to punishing those who commit grievous evil. Its paladins mark a chosen enemy with Vow of Enmity to gain the upper hand against it, and pursue their quarry relentlessly. They set aside their own purity if that is what it takes to destroy evil.",
  "dnd.s.ranger.beastmaster": "The Beast Master is a Ranger archetype bonded to an animal companion. Ranger and beast fight together as a team; in the newer rules the companion is a primal beast of land, sea or sky that the ranger calls forth. Their mutual trust grows deeper as they advance.",
  "dnd.s.ranger.gloomstalker": "The Gloom Stalker is a Ranger archetype at home in the darkest places, such as the Underdark. They strike first and hardest at the start of combat with Dread Ambusher, and become nearly invisible to creatures that rely on darkvision. Their minds are also hardened against fear and intrusion.",
  "dnd.s.ranger.hunter": "The Hunter is a Ranger archetype that stands between civilisation and the terrors of the wild. Hunters choose specialised techniques against their prey, such as cutting through hordes, felling giants or evading attacks. They are versatile defenders who adapt to whatever threat they face.",
  "dnd.s.rogue.arcanetrickster": "The Arcane Trickster is a Rogue archetype that adds illusion and enchantment magic to stealth and agility. They use an invisible Mage Hand to pick pockets and locks from a distance, and later use spells to confuse and ambush their targets. Their magic serves the art of deception.",
  "dnd.s.rogue.inquisitive": "The Inquisitive is a Rogue archetype for detectives who expose secrets and lies. Their keen senses catch falsehoods and hidden details, and they can read a foe closely enough to find an opening for Sneak Attack. They excel at investigation, interrogation and unravelling mysteries.",
  "dnd.s.rogue.swashbuckler": "The Swashbuckler is a Rogue archetype of daring duellists who rely on speed, elegance and charm. They slip away from foes after attacking, can Sneak Attack while fighting one-on-one, and use their Panache to captivate or provoke others. They are the dashing heroes of adventure stories.",
  "dnd.s.sorcerer.aberrant": "Aberrant Sorcery is a Sorcerer origin whose power comes from an alien influence, such as the Far Realm, mind flayers or a strange psionic event. These sorcerers speak telepathically, learn psionic spells they can cast subtly, and eventually reshape their bodies in unsettling ways. It first appeared as the Aberrant Mind in Tasha's Cauldron of Everything.",
  "dnd.s.sorcerer.draconic": "Draconic Sorcery is a Sorcerer origin whose magic comes from the blood of dragons. These sorcerers have naturally tough, scaly skin, empower spells of their draconic ancestor's element, and eventually sprout dragon wings and radiate an aura of awe. Their personalities often echo the pride of dragons.",
  "dnd.s.sorcerer.storm": "Storm Sorcery is a Sorcerer origin powered by elemental air. These sorcerers are wrapped in wind and lightning, can fly a short distance when casting spells, and later resist lightning and thunder while bending the weather to their will. Their power is often traced to a great storm or to the Elemental Plane of Air.",
  "dnd.s.sorcerer.wildmagic": "Wild Magic Sorcery is a Sorcerer origin whose power comes from the chaotic forces of raw magic. Casting spells can trigger an unpredictable Wild Magic Surge that may be helpful or bizarre, and these sorcerers can bend luck with Tides of Chaos. Their magic is thrilling but never fully under control.",
  "dnd.s.warlock.archfey": "The Archfey Patron is a Warlock patron that ties the warlock to a powerful lord or lady of the fey. These warlocks charm or frighten others and slip away from danger with misty, teleporting steps. Their patrons are capricious beings of the Feywild with alien sensibilities and whims.",
  "dnd.s.warlock.celestial": "The Celestial Patron is a Warlock patron that binds the warlock to a being of the Upper Planes, such as an empyrean or a unicorn. These warlocks heal with Healing Light, deal radiant and fire damage, and later can rise again from the brink of death. Their power resembles a cleric's but flows from a personal bond.",
  "dnd.s.warlock.fiend": "The Fiend Patron is a Warlock patron that offers a pact with a being of the Lower Planes, such as a demon lord or an archdevil. These warlocks gain temporary hit points when they bring down enemies, call on dark luck and grow resistant to harm. Such bargains are dangerous and never truly free.",
  "dnd.s.warlock.greatoldone": "The Great Old One Patron is a Warlock patron whose power comes from an unknowable entity far beyond the planes. These warlocks gain telepathy, guard their thoughts and can bend the minds of others. Their patron may not even know they exist, granting forbidden knowledge rather than guidance.",
  "dnd.s.wizard.enchantment": "The School of Enchantment is a Wizard tradition specialising in magic that influences minds and behaviour. These wizards can entrance enemies with their gaze, divert attacks and later alter the memories of those they charm. They hold that persuasion and control can settle conflicts without bloodshed.",
  "dnd.s.wizard.illusion": "The Illusionist is a Wizard specialised in deceptive magic that fools the senses. Their illusions are especially convincing, can be reshaped after casting and can even become partly real. They also protect themselves with illusory doubles that confuse attackers.",
  "dnd.s.wizard.transmutation": "The School of Transmutation is a Wizard tradition specialising in magic that alters matter and energy. These wizards can temporarily turn one material into another, craft a transmuter's stone that grants various benefits and change their own shape. At their peak they can work miraculous transformations.",
  "dnd.al.CE": "Chaotic Evil characters act with arbitrary violence, driven by greed, hatred or bloodlust. They reject order and usually hold together only out of fear of a stronger leader. The demons of the Abyss and red dragons are the classic Player's Handbook examples.",
  "dnd.al.CG": "Chaotic Good characters follow their own conscience with little regard for what others expect. They value freedom and kindness and distrust rules that stand in the way of doing good. Copper dragons, many elves and unicorns are the Player's Handbook examples.",
  "dnd.al.CN": "Chaotic Neutral characters follow their whims and value personal freedom above all else. They are moved neither by malice nor by altruism, but by independence. According to the Player's Handbook, many barbarians and rogues and some bards lean this way.",
  "dnd.al.LE": "Lawful Evil characters take what they want methodically, within the limits of a code, a tradition or a hierarchy. They honor contracts and loyalty as long as it serves them. The devils of the Nine Hells are the archetypal example, alongside tyrants and scheming officials.",
  "dnd.al.LG": "Lawful Good characters try to do the right thing in the way society expects: they keep their word, respect legitimate authority and protect the innocent. Paladins and crusading knights are the classic picture. Its typical weakness is rigidity, mistaking the rules for goodness itself.",
  "dnd.al.LN": "Lawful Neutral characters act according to law, tradition or a personal code rather than out of kindness or cruelty. Order itself is the value. Judges, disciplined soldiers and many monks fit this alignment.",
  "dnd.al.NE": "Neutral Evil characters do whatever they can get away with, purely out of self-interest, without loyalty to law or love of chaos. It is selfishness in its purest form. The mercenary fiends known as yugoloths are the archetypal neutral evil creatures.",
  "dnd.al.NG": "Neutral Good characters do the most good they can, without a strong bias for or against rules. They help wherever help is needed, working with authority or around it as the situation demands. Many celestials and most gnomes are listed as neutral good in the Player's Handbook.",
  "dnd.al.NN": "True Neutral characters stay away from moral and ethical extremes, either avoiding hard choices or deliberately seeking balance. Some druids see neutrality as harmony with the natural order. The Player's Handbook lists most druids and many humans as neutral.",
  "dnd.r.changeling": "Changelings are shapeshifters who can change their appearance and voice at will, taking on a new face whenever they wish. They come from the Eberron setting and later appeared in Mordenkainen Presents: Monsters of the Multiverse. Their true form is pale with colorless eyes, and many changelings keep several personas for different situations.",
  "dnd.r.dragonborn": "Dragonborn are proud humanoids shaped by draconic ancestry, with scaled bodies and dragon-like heads. Each is linked to a kind of dragon, which sets the damage type of their breath weapon and their resistance: acid, cold, fire, lightning or poison. Clan and honor matter greatly to them, and in the Forgotten Realms they are tied to the nation of Tymanther.",
  "dnd.r.drow": "Drow are the dark elves of the Underdark, the vast world beneath the surface. Traditionally they live in matriarchal cities such as Menzoberranzan, ruled by priestesses of the spider goddess Lolth. They have superior darkvision and innate magic such as faerie fire and darkness. Drizzt Do'Urden, who left his people for the surface, is the most famous drow.",
  "dnd.r.dwarf": "Dwarves are sturdy, long-lived folk famous as miners, smiths and builders of halls carved into mountains. They are tough, resist poison and see in the dark. Clan, ancestry and old grudges weigh heavily in their culture, and Moradin the Soul Forger heads the dwarven pantheon.",
  "dnd.r.elf": "Elves are graceful, long-lived people with a deep bond to magic and the Feywild. Instead of sleeping, they rest in a meditative trance for a few hours. Their fey ancestry helps them resist being charmed. There are many elven lineages, including high elves, wood elves and drow.",
  "dnd.r.goliath": "Goliaths are towering people related to giants, traditionally dwelling in high mountains. In the 2024 Player's Handbook each goliath has a giant ancestry that grants a supernatural boon, such as fire, frost or storm power, and can briefly grow to Large size. Their culture values fair competition and personal achievement.",
  "dnd.r.halfling": "Halflings are small, cheerful folk who treasure home, good food and close-knit communities. They are famous for their luck, which lets them reroll a natural 1. They are brave for their size and nimble enough to slip past larger creatures. Many live in quiet villages of their own or among humans.",
  "dnd.r.halforc": "Half-Orcs combine human and orc heritage and were a playable race in the 2014 Player's Handbook. They are known for strength and endurance: when struck down, they can grit their teeth and stay on their feet with 1 hit point. Their savage attacks deal extra damage on critical hits. In the 2024 rules the orc species takes their place.",
  "dnd.r.human": "Humans are the most widespread and varied people in most D&D worlds. Their lives are short compared to those of elves or dwarves, which pushes them toward ambition and quick achievement. In the 2024 rules they are known for resourcefulness and versatility, gaining Heroic Inspiration after every long rest and an extra skill.",
  "dnd.r.kenku": "Kenku are flightless, crow-like bird people. They are famous for mimicry, reproducing sounds and voices they have heard. They also have a keen memory for details. Older lore tells that they lost their wings and their own voices as punishment for an ancient betrayal.",
  "dnd.r.tabaxi": "Tabaxi are cat-like humanoids who, in the Forgotten Realms, come from the distant land of Maztica. They are driven by curiosity and love collecting stories, objects and experiences. Their claws help them climb, their feline agility lets them sprint in short bursts, and they see well in dim light.",
  "dnd.r.tiefling": "Tieflings carry a fiendish legacy in their blood, often visible as horns, a tail and unusual skin or eye colors. In the Forgotten Realms many trace this heritage to Asmodeus, lord of the Nine Hells. The 2024 rules offer abyssal, chthonic and infernal legacies, each with its own resistance and innate spells. They often face mistrust, but their heritage does not decide their character.",
  "dnd.r.yuanti": "Yuan-ti are serpent folk descended from humans transformed by ancient rituals devoted to snake gods. Their empires once ruled the jungles, and many still dream of restoring them. As player characters in Monsters of the Multiverse they resist magic and poison and have innate magic that can charm snakes.",
  "wi.g.aard": "Aard is a sign that releases a telekinetic blast, knocking back, staggering or stunning opponents. Witchers also use it to break walls, doors and other obstacles. In the games it is one of the five basic signs, and its upgraded forms can strike enemies all around the witcher.",
  "wi.g.axii": "Axii is a sign that affects the mind, calming, confusing or charming its target. In the books Geralt uses it to soothe animals or briefly bend a person's will. In the games it can stop an enemy from fighting or even turn it against its allies, and it opens extra dialogue options.",
  "wi.g.igni": "Igni is a sign that releases a burst of fire, burning enemies and setting objects alight. It is especially effective against creatures vulnerable to flame. In The Witcher 3 its advanced form produces a continuous stream of fire.",
  "wi.g.quen": "Quen is a protective sign that surrounds the witcher with a magical shield that absorbs damage. It is the witcher's standard defense in dangerous fights. In The Witcher 3 an alternate form creates an active shield that restores the witcher's health as it absorbs blows.",
  "wi.g.yrden": "Yrden is a sign that draws a magic glyph on the ground, slowing enemies who step into it. It is crucial against specters and other incorporeal beings, which become tangible inside the circle. In The Witcher 3 an alternate form turns it into a magic trap that strikes nearby enemies.",
  "wi.s.bear": "The School of the Bear is known for heavy armor and powerful, enduring witchers who could take a great deal of punishment. Its keep is called Haern Caduch. Bear witchers had a reputation for living apart from others and for a grim, solitary temperament.",
  "wi.s.cat": "The School of the Cat produced agile, fast fighters in light armor who favored speed over defense. Its witchers have a dark reputation, and many took work as assassins or mercenaries. Some sources describe the school as having no permanent keep and traveling from place to place. Gaetan in The Witcher 3 is one of its members.",
  "wi.s.griffin": "The School of the Griffin puts more weight on signs than other schools, and its witchers are known for a knightly code of conduct. Its keep, Kaer Seren, stood on the coast. Coën, Geralt's friend in the books, trained there. Its gear in The Witcher 3 is medium armor suited to a sign-based style.",
  "wi.s.manticore": "The School of the Manticore is one of the lesser-known witcher schools, mentioned mainly in supplementary material such as the tabletop RPG. Few details about its keep, members and methods are firmly established in canon, so descriptions vary between sources. Like the other schools, it is named after a fearsome beast.",
  "wi.s.viper": "The School of the Viper is associated with the southern lands and with the Nilfgaardian Empire. Its most famous member is Letho of Gulet, the Kingslayer of The Witcher 2. Its keep is called Gorthur Gvaed, and its witchers are linked with poisons and light, agile gear.",
  "wi.s.wolf": "The School of the Wolf trained its witchers at Kaer Morhen, a keep in the Blue Mountains of Kaedwen. It is the school of Geralt of Rivia, together with Vesemir, Eskel and Lambert. The keep was attacked long ago, and only a handful of witchers remained afterward. Wolf witchers balance swordsmanship, signs and alchemy.",
  "av.e.water": "Water is the element of change. Waterbenders, found mostly in the Northern and Southern Water Tribes, learned their art from the Moon and the push and pull of the ocean. Their power grows under the full moon and fades at the new moon. Waterbending includes advanced techniques such as healing, plantbending and bloodbending.",
  "av.e.earth": "Earth is the element of substance. Earthbenders, most common in the Earth Kingdom, learned from the badgermoles, the original earthbenders. Their style relies on standing firm and waiting for the right moment to strike. Its sub-skills include metalbending, sandbending and lavabending.",
  "av.e.fire": "Fire is the element of power. Firebenders, mostly from the Fire Nation, originally learned from the dragons, and their strength comes from breath and inner energy. Firebending grows stronger with the sun and vanishes during a solar eclipse. Advanced forms include lightning generation and combustionbending.",
  "av.e.air": "Air is the element of freedom. Airbenders learned from the sky bison and lived as the Air Nomads in four temples. After the Fire Nation's attack on the Nomads, Aang was the last airbender, until new airbenders appeared after Harmonic Convergence. Airbending is evasive and defensive, built on circular movement.",
  "av.t.vines": "Plantbending is a waterbending technique that controls the water inside plants, letting a bender move vines, roots and other plant matter. It was developed by the Foggy Swamp Tribe. It shows that waterbending can work with living things, not just open water.",
  "av.t.sand": "Sandbending is an earthbending sub-skill for controlling loose sand. It was developed by the sandbenders of the Si Wong Desert, who use it to drive sand-sailers across the dunes and to raise sandstorms. It requires finer control than ordinary earthbending.",
  "av.t.healing": "Healing is a waterbending skill in which the bender guides water along the body's energy paths to mend wounds and ease pain. Katara is a gifted healer. Water from the Spirit Oasis at the North Pole has special healing power, as when Katara used it to save Aang.",
  "av.t.metal": "Metalbending is an earthbending technique invented by Toph Beifong, who sensed traces of earth inside metal and learned to bend it. Very pure metals such as platinum cannot be bent. By the time of The Legend of Korra it had become widespread, used by the Republic City police and the metal clan of Zaofu.",
  "av.t.lightning": "Lightning generation is an advanced firebending skill, sometimes called cold-blooded fire, in which the bender separates positive and negative energy and lets them crash back together. Azula, Ozai and Iroh mastered it. Iroh also created a technique for redirecting lightning through the body's energy paths.",
  "av.t.blood": "Bloodbending is a waterbending technique that controls the water inside a living body, taking over the target's movements. Hama developed it as a prisoner of the Fire Nation and used it under the full moon. It was later outlawed; in The Legend of Korra, Yakone and his sons could bloodbend even without the full moon.",
  "av.t.lava": "Lavabending is a rare earthbending sub-skill that turns rock into molten lava and back again. In The Legend of Korra, Ghazan of the Red Lotus and later Bolin mastered it. Some Avatars, such as Roku, could use it as well.",
  "av.t.combustion": "Combustionbending is a rare firebending sub-skill in which the bender creates explosions by focusing energy through the mind. Its users usually bear a third-eye tattoo on the forehead. The best-known combustionbenders are Combustion Man and P'Li of the Red Lotus.",
  "av.t.spirit": "Energybending is the oldest form of bending and existed before the four elements were bent. The lion turtles used it to grant people the power of the elements. Aang used it to take away Ozai's bending, and it later played a part in Korra restoring bending to others.",
  "av.avatar": "The Avatar is the master of all four elements and the bridge between the human world and the Spirit World. The cycle began with Wan, who merged with Raava, the spirit of light and peace. When an Avatar dies, they are reborn in the next nation of the cycle: Fire, Air, Water, Earth. In the Avatar State they can draw on the power and knowledge of their past lives.",
  "wod.aus.ahroun": "Ahroun are Garou born under the full moon, the warriors of the Garou Nation. Their Rage burns hotter than that of any other auspice, and they are expected to stand on the front line against the Wyrm and its servants. Their Gifts favour battle, endurance and command in combat. Many fall fighting, and those who survive carry the memory of every packmate they have lost.",
  "wod.aus.galliard": "Born under the gibbous moon, Galliards are the moon-dancers of the Garou: storytellers, poets and keepers of oral history. They preserve their tribe's legends, lead the great howls at moots and stir their packs before battle. Their Gifts deal with communication, emotion and dreams. Passionate by nature, they swing as easily into celebration as into fury.",
  "wod.aus.philodox": "Philodox are born under the half moon, balanced between light and darkness, and serve as the judges and lawkeepers of the Garou. They know the Litany by heart, settle disputes, oversee challenges and weigh claims to Renown. Their Gifts help them discern truth and read the intentions of others. Respected and sometimes resented, they are expected to stay impartial even when their own pack is involved.",
  "wod.aus.ragabash": "Born under the new moon, Ragabash are the tricksters and questioners of the Garou Nation. Their sacred duty is to challenge tradition, expose hypocrisy and keep elders from growing complacent, often by way of pranks. Their Gifts favour stealth, cunning and misdirection. They have the least Rage of any auspice, yet they are valued as scouts and as the voice that asks uncomfortable questions.",
  "wod.aus.theurge": "Theurges are born under the crescent moon and are the seers and spirit-talkers of the Garou. They commune with the spirits of the Umbra, bind them into fetishes and perform many of the rites. Their Gnosis runs high and their Gifts lean toward the mystical. Often thought strange or distant, they are nonetheless vital to the survival of every pack.",
  "wod.breed.homid": "Homids are Garou born to human parents and raised among humans, usually unaware of their heritage until the First Change. Today they are the most numerous breed, and they understand human society and technology far better than their kin. Their natural form is human, and they begin with the least Gnosis. Some Garou, the Red Talons above all, regard them with suspicion.",
  "wod.breed.lupus": "Lupus Garou are born to wolves and live as part of a wolf pack until their First Change. Their natural form is the wolf, and they share the closest bond with Gaia and the spirit world, reflected in their high Gnosis. Human concepts such as money, writing or clock time often baffle them. As wild wolves dwindle, so does this breed.",
  "wod.breed.metis": "These Garou are born of a union between two werewolves, a union forbidden by the Litany. They enter the world in Crinos, the war form, cannot have offspring of their own, and each bears a physical deformity that betrays their origin. Long treated as outcasts and a source of shame, they nonetheless grow up within the sept and often know Garou society and the Umbra more intimately than anyone. Their natural form is Crinos.",
  "wod.gen": "Generation counts how many Embraces separate a vampire from Caine, the first of the Kindred. The lower the generation, the more potent the blood: it allows higher Traits, a larger blood pool and more blood spent per turn. Recently Embraced neonates are commonly of the Twelfth or Thirteenth Generation, while the Antediluvians of the Third Generation are near-mythical. Diablerie, devouring the soul of a vampire of lower generation, is the only way to lower one's own.",
  "wod.gnosis": "Gnosis is a Garou's spiritual connection to Gaia and the Umbra. It powers many Gifts, lets a werewolf step sideways into the spirit world and is needed to activate fetishes and talens. Lupus and Theurges tend to have the most. Gnosis and Rage pull against each other, since fury makes spiritual focus difficult.",
  "wod.path.accord": "The Path of Honorable Accord is a Sabbat Path of Enlightenment built on chivalry, loyalty and keeping one's word. Its followers, the Knights, believe that honour and duty to sect, pack and leaders are what hold the Beast at bay. It is popular among the sect's warriors and guardians. Breaking an oath or betraying a comrade is the gravest of sins.",
  "wod.path.blood": "The Path of Blood is the traditional moral code of the Assamites, rooted in the teachings of their founder Haqim. Its followers regard other Kindred as accursed and believe that taking their blood, above all through diablerie, brings them closer to Haqim. It demands discipline, loyalty to the clan and the judgement of Caine's unworthy descendants.",
  "wod.path.caine": "Followers of the Path of Caine, called Noddists, study the legends of the first vampire as preserved in the Book of Nod. They see vampirism not as a curse to escape but as a condition to understand and master, and they model themselves on Caine. They are scholars, debaters and seekers of ancient lore, and they consider diablerie and a deliberate use of the Beast to be acceptable tools. The path is most common in the Sabbat.",
  "wod.path.cathari": "The Path of Cathari draws on the medieval Cathar heresy, which held that the material world is the work of evil. Its followers see themselves as damned spirits whose role is to embody indulgence and temptation, and they pursue passion and excess openly rather than restraining it. They still keep a code of their own, choosing carefully whom to tempt and whom to Embrace.",
  "wod.path.feral": "The Path of the Feral Heart teaches that vampires are predators and that the Beast is a natural part of them rather than an enemy. Its followers live by the instincts of the hunter: they kill when hungry, defend their territory and shun needless cruelty. It is especially popular among the Gangrel and other Kindred close to the wild, and it is associated with the Sabbat.",
  "wod.path.humanity": "Humanity is the moral compass most Kindred cling to, a measure of how much of their mortal conscience survives the Embrace. It follows ordinary human ethics: not murdering, not stealing, respecting others. As Humanity falls, a vampire grows colder, more monstrous and more vulnerable to the Beast. Most Camarilla vampires follow it, while many in the Sabbat abandon it for a Path of Enlightenment.",
  "wod.path.lilith": "Followers of the Path of Lilith, the Bahari, revere Lilith, Caine's first wife, who in their legends taught him the secrets of the night. They believe pain is the great teacher and that suffering brings growth and wisdom. Their rites are mystical and secretive, and many Kindred regard them as dangerous heretics who contradict the Book of Nod.",
  "wod.path.metamorph": "The Path of Metamorphosis belongs above all to the Tzimisce, especially their elders and scholars. Its followers see vampirism as a stage of transformation to be transcended, and they use the Discipline of Vicissitude to reshape their bodies and study the nature of the Kindred. They regard emotion as an irrelevant distraction and pursue knowledge without indulging others or themselves.",
  "wod.path.night": "The Path of Night, created by the Lasombra, holds that vampires are damned by God and that their role is to be the darkness that tests humanity. Its followers, the Nihilists, spread fear among mortals so that only the truly faithful find salvation. They see themselves as instruments of a divine plan rather than mere monsters. It is followed mostly by the Lasombra and others in the Sabbat.",
  "wod.path.paradox": "The Path of Paradox is the traditional ethos of the Ravnos, drawing on Indian concepts such as samsara and svadharma. Its followers believe that vampires have fallen out of the cycle of rebirth and that their duty is to pierce illusion and restore the proper flow of the cosmos, sometimes by removing those who obstruct it. It is little known outside the clan.",
  "wod.path.power": "Followers of the Path of Power and the Inner Voice, the Unifiers, believe enlightenment comes through mastering oneself and accumulating power in the world. They are the Sabbat's ambitious politicians and strategists, valuing results above sentiment and using fear and pain as tools of control. Mercy and failure are both seen as weakness. Many Lasombra and Sabbat leaders walk this path.",
  "wod.path.scorched": "The Path of the Scorched Heart, whose followers are called the Unforgiving, teaches that emotions are tools of the Beast and must be extinguished by cold reason. It is associated with the True Brujah and with the secretive Tal'Mahe'Ra. Its adherents dismiss morality as an emotional construct while keeping a strict ethical code, value fact over opinion and make use of others whenever it profits them.",
  "wod.path.self": "The Path of Self-Focus is followed by the Internalists and is associated with the secretive Tal'Mahe'Ra. Drawing on Asian spiritual traditions, it teaches that the world cannot be changed, only oneself. Its followers avoid meddling in the affairs of others, meditate regularly, live in the present and seek self-mastery to keep the Beast in check.",
  "wod.path.typhon": "The Path of Typhon is the orthodox faith of the Followers of Set. Its adherents, the Theophidians, believe that Set is a god who will one day return, and they work to free mortals and Kindred from the chains of convention, conscience and faith through temptation and corruption. They study Set's mysteries and hold that aiding his resurrection is the highest duty.",
  "wod.rage": "Rage is the fury of Gaia's warriors, the burning anger that fuels a Garou's strength in battle. It lets a werewolf act with supernatural speed, change shape in an instant and fight on through wounds, but it is also dangerous: too much Rage drives a Garou into frenzy and unsettles humans and animals alike. Ahroun carry the most Rage, Ragabash the least.",
  "wod.rank.adren": "Adren is the third rank of Garou society, reached by those who have gathered great Renown. Adren are seasoned veterans, often pack leaders or sept officers, and their words carry weight at moots. They are expected to teach and guide those of lower rank.",
  "wod.rank.athro": "Athro is the fourth rank, attained by relatively few Garou. Athro are renowned heroes whose deeds are told in their tribe's songs, and they often hold important posts in the sept, such as warder or master of the rite. Their judgement is widely respected.",
  "wod.rank.cliath": "Cliath is the first true rank of Garou society, earned by completing the Rite of Passage. A cliath is accepted as a full member of the tribe but has yet to prove themselves, and most new characters begin here. They earn Renown by fulfilling the duties of their auspice and are expected to heed the guidance of their elders.",
  "wod.rank.elder": "Elder is the fifth rank, the highest commonly reached in Garou society. Elders are legendary figures who lead septs and tribes and shape the course of the Garou Nation; their word carries enormous weight. Only a handful of Garou ever reach this rank.",
  "wod.rank.fostern": "Fostern is the second rank of the Garou, granted to those whose deeds have earned enough Renown to prove their worth. Fostern have experience behind them and are trusted with more important tasks, such as leading a pack on a mission. They are respected warriors and hunters but not yet among the leaders of the sept.",
  "wod.sect.cam": "The Camarilla is the largest sect of the Kindred, a society that claims to speak for all vampires. At its heart is the Masquerade, the tradition of hiding the existence of vampires from mortals, along with the other Traditions enforced by a prince in each city. The Ventrue, Toreador, Tremere, Nosferatu, Malkavians and Brujah form its backbone. It values order, stability and the authority of elders.",
  "wod.sect.sab": "The Sabbat is a sect of vampires that rejects the Masquerade's humility and the rule of the Antediluvians, holding the Kindred to be superior to humanity. Its members gather in packs bound by the Vaulderie, a shared blood ritual that replaces individual blood bonds with loyalty to the sect. Led by the Lasombra and Tzimisce, it wages war on the Camarilla and prepares for the return of the Antediluvians. Many of its members follow Paths of Enlightenment instead of Humanity.",
  "wod.vamp.assamite": "The Assamites are a clan of assassins, warriors and scholars from the Middle East, descended from Haqim. Their clan Disciplines are Celerity, Obfuscate and Quietus, an art of deadly blood. Long known for accepting contracts on the lives of other Kindred, often paid in vitae, they once laboured under a Tremere curse that restricted their diablerie. The clan is divided into castes of warriors, viziers and sorcerers and stands apart from the major sects.",
  "wod.vamp.brujah": "The Brujah were once philosopher-kings of ancient Carthage; today they are known as rebels, idealists and agitators. Their Disciplines are Celerity, Potence and Presence. Their passion is legendary, and it makes them prone to frenzy. Most belong to the Camarilla while remaining its most restless members; others join the Anarchs or the Sabbat.",
  "wod.vamp.gangrel": "The Gangrel are wanderers who feel more at home in the wilderness than in the cities. Their Disciplines are Animalism, Fortitude and Protean, which lets them grow claws, sink into the earth and take animal shapes. Each frenzy can leave them with a lasting animal feature. Independent by nature, they have drifted away from the Camarilla, and many keep company with the Anarchs or the Sabbat.",
  "wod.vamp.giovanni": "The Giovanni are a family clan of Venetian merchants and necromancers. Their Disciplines are Dominate, Necromancy and Potence. They Embrace mostly from within their own bloodline, hold great wealth in the mortal world and command the spirits of the dead. They rose to power by committing diablerie on their own Antediluvian, and they now stand independent, bound by an old pact with the Camarilla to keep to themselves.",
  "wod.vamp.lasombra": "The Lasombra are proud aristocrats of shadow and the leaders of the Sabbat. Their Disciplines are Dominate, Obtenebration and Potence; Obtenebration lets them command living darkness. They cast no reflection in mirrors. Many have deep ties to the Church, and they prize ambition, excellence and leadership.",
  "wod.vamp.malkavian": "The Malkavians are the madmen of the Kindred: every member of the clan carries a derangement from the moment of the Embrace. Their Disciplines are Auspex, Dementation and Obfuscate, though some lines practise Dominate in place of Dementation. Their madness grants them strange insight and a unique perception of truth, and many believe they share a hidden link known as the Malkavian Madness Network. Most belong to the Camarilla, while a faction serves the Sabbat.",
  "wod.vamp.nosferatu": "The Nosferatu are hideously disfigured by the Embrace and must hide their faces from mortals. Their Disciplines are Animalism, Obfuscate and Potence. They make their havens in sewers and tunnels beneath the cities and are the finest information brokers among the Kindred. Despite their appearance they are often loyal and practical, and most belong to the Camarilla.",
  "wod.vamp.setite": "The Followers of Set are a clan of Egyptian origin who worship their Antediluvian, Set, as a god. Their Disciplines are Obfuscate, Presence and Serpentis, which grants them serpentine traits. They are known as tempters and dealers in vice and secrets, believing that corruption frees souls from their chains. They stand independent of the sects and are widely distrusted by other Kindred.",
  "wod.vamp.toreador": "The Toreador are artists, aesthetes and lovers of beauty. Their Disciplines are Auspex, Celerity and Presence. They are prominent in the Camarilla, where they shape Elysium and the cultural life of the city. Their love of beauty is also their weakness: something truly beautiful can entrance them until they forget everything else.",
  "wod.vamp.tremere": "The Tremere were mortal wizards who seized immortality through blood magic. Their Disciplines are Auspex, Dominate and Thaumaturgy. They are rigidly organised in a Pyramid ruled from Vienna, and members are bound to the clan's elders by blood. They are a pillar of the Camarilla, valued for their sorcery but distrusted for their ambition.",
  "wod.vamp.tzimisce": "The Tzimisce are old-world aristocrats of Eastern Europe, scholars and masters of flesh. Their Disciplines are Animalism, Auspex and Vicissitude, which lets them sculpt flesh and bone. They must rest in the earth of their homeland. Together with the Lasombra they lead the Sabbat, and many of them pursue the Path of Metamorphosis.",
  "wod.vamp.ventrue": "The Ventrue are the clan of kings, the aristocrats and leaders of the Camarilla. Their Disciplines are Dominate, Fortitude and Presence. They see themselves as the guardians of Kindred society and often hold the title of prince. Their weakness is a refined palate: each Ventrue can feed only on one particular kind of mortal.",
  "wod.wolf.fangs": "The Silver Fangs are the traditional leaders of the Garou Nation, a tribe of noble lineage that prizes pure bloodlines. They have ruled the Garou for ages, valuing honour, purity and nobility. Generations of inbreeding, however, have left many of them with derangements, and their authority is increasingly contested. Their Gifts favour leadership, dignity and inspiring others.",
  "wod.wolf.fenris": "The Get of Fenris are fierce warriors of Norse and Germanic origin who live for battle against the Wyrm. They value strength, courage and glory, and treat weakness as a vice. Their Gifts favour combat, endurance and intimidation. Respected for their prowess, they are also criticised for their harshness and intolerance.",
  "wod.wolf.fianna": "The Fianna are Celtic Garou, the bards and storytellers of the Nation. They are known for their passion, their love of song and drink, and their deep bond with the fae. Their Gifts favour song, illusion and the faerie world. Loyal friends and fierce enemies, they keep the oldest tales of the Garou alive.",
  "wod.wolf.gaia": "The Children of Gaia are the peacemakers of the Garou Nation, working to unite the tribes and heal old wounds. They seek harmony with humanity and believe diplomacy and compassion matter as much as battle in the war against the Wyrm. Their Gifts favour healing, calm and protection. Others sometimes call them naive, yet they fight fiercely when they must.",
  "wod.wolf.glass": "The Glass Walkers are urban Garou who have embraced cities and technology. They move through business, computing and modern society, believing the Garou must adapt to the modern world in order to survive. Their Gifts favour technology, the urban landscape and the digital Umbra. Other tribes often suspect them of standing too close to the Weaver.",
  "wod.wolf.gnawers": "The Bone Gnawers are the tribe of the streets, living among the homeless and the poor of the cities. Looked down on by the other tribes, they are resourceful, humble survivors who protect those society has forgotten. Their Gifts favour survival, scavenging and hiding. Few know more about what happens in a city than they do.",
  "wod.wolf.shadow": "The Shadow Lords are ambitious, calculating Garou with roots in Eastern Europe, who believe that strength and cunning should decide who leads. They have long challenged the Silver Fangs for leadership of the Nation. Their Gifts favour intimidation, manipulation and the power of storms. Feared and distrusted, they are nonetheless disciplined and effective.",
  "wod.wolf.stargazers": "The Stargazers are contemplative Garou with roots in Asia who seek inner balance through meditation and philosophy. They are known for their martial arts and spiritual insight. Their Gifts favour clarity, perception and self-control. At times they have distanced themselves from the Garou Nation and drawn closer to the shapeshifters of the East.",
  "wod.wolf.striders": "The Silent Striders are wanderers of Egyptian origin, driven from their homeland long ago and fated to travel without rest. They are messengers, scouts and explorers, roaming both the physical world and the Umbra. Their Gifts favour speed, travel and dealings with the dead. Solitary by nature, they are among the best-informed of the Garou.",
  "wod.wolf.talons": "The Red Talons are a tribe made up entirely of Lupus Garou, who see humanity as the greatest threat to Gaia. They are fierce defenders of the wilderness and despise human cities and technology. Their Gifts favour nature, the senses and tearing down human works. Many of them long for the day when human numbers fall to what the earth can bear.",
  "wod.wolf.uktena": "The Uktena are mystics and keepers of secrets with roots among the indigenous peoples of the Americas, though they also accept members from many other marginalised cultures. They seek out and bind the dark spirits of the world, delving into forbidden lore so that others cannot misuse it. Their Gifts favour magic, the occult and hidden knowledge. Other tribes value their wisdom but fear the risks they take.",
  "wod.wolf.wendigo": "The Wendigo are a fierce tribe rooted among the indigenous peoples of North America. They harbour deep resentment toward the European tribes for past injustices and defend their lands and peoples with fierce loyalty. Their Gifts favour cold, wind and the spirits of winter. Their totem is the great spirit Wendigo.",
  "wod.banality": "Banality is the chilling force of disbelief, routine and cold reason that slowly kills the Dreaming. Every changeling carries some of it, and the more Banality one has, the harder it is to work cantrips and to remember one's fae nature. Banal mortals and places can unravel faerie magic and drive changelings into forgetfulness. Unchecked, it ends in the Undoing, when the fae soul fades away and only the mortal remains.",
  "wod.court.seelie": "The Seelie Court is the court of summer, which by tradition rules faerie society from Beltaine to Samhain. Its members uphold honor, chivalry, tradition and noblesse oblige, and its ideals include \"Death before dishonor\" and \"Love conquers all\". Most of the sidhe nobility and the High King's realm follow Seelie ways. Every changeling has both a Seelie and an Unseelie side, and the Seelie side is the one that keeps promises and protects the weak.",
  "wod.court.unseelie": "The Unseelie Court is the court of winter, which by tradition holds sway from Samhain to Beltaine. It values passion, freedom and change over duty and custom, with ideals such as \"Change is good\" and \"Passion before duty\". Unseelie fae are not simply evil, but they distrust rigid honor and follow their own desires. Its more radical, hidden wing is the Shadow Court, which plots to overturn the Seelie order.",
  "wod.demon.a.defilers": "Lammasu is the original angelic name of the House of the Defilers, the Fifth House, often called the Angels of the Deep. In the beginning they ruled the seas and waters and stirred humanity's hearts toward beauty, truth and inspiration. Their Lores are Longing, Storms and Transfiguration. After the Fall, their gift for inspiring desire turned toward temptation and manipulation, which earned them their mortal name.",
  "wod.demon.a.devils": "Namaru is the original angelic name of the House of Devils, the First House, the Heralds of Heaven. They were God's standard-bearers and voices, carrying the Creator's will to the rest of the Host and leading the angels. Lucifer himself belonged to this house, and many Namaru became the generals of the rebellion. Their Lores are the Celestials, Flame and Radiance.",
  "wod.demon.a.devourers": "Rabisu is the original angelic name of the House of the Devourers, the Sixth House. Its angels created and tended the plants and animals of the world, filling living things with vitality and instinct. Their Lores are the Beast, the Wild and Flesh. During the war against Heaven they learned to reshape living bodies into weapons, and the hunger and savagery of those days gave them their mortal name.",
  "wod.demon.a.fiends": "Neberu is the original angelic name of the House of the Fiends, the Fourth House, the angels of the heavens and stars. They set the cosmos running and read the great pattern of its motions, foreseeing how events would unfold. Their Lores are Light, Patterns and Portals. After the Fall they became masters of prophecy, illusion and fate, which mortals came to fear.",
  "wod.demon.a.malefactors": "Annunaki is the original angelic name of the House of the Malefactors, the Third House, the Artificers of creation. They shaped the stone, soil and metal of the world and made the tools its creatures would use. Their Lores are the Earth, Paths and the Forge. During the war they turned their craft to forging weapons and terrible artifacts, which earned them their mortal name.",
  "wod.demon.a.scourges": "Asharu is the original angelic name of the House of the Scourges, the Second House, the Angels of the Firmament. They breathed life into newborn creatures, governed the winds and the air, and watched over humanity as protectors and healers. Their Lores are Awakening, the Firmament and the Winds. After the Fall the same powers could spread sickness and suffering instead of health.",
  "wod.demon.a.slayers": "Halaku is the original angelic name of the House of the Slayers, the Seventh House, the Angels of Death. Their duty was to end what had run its course and to return it to creation so that it could begin again. Their Lores are Death, the Spirit and the Realms. When humanity lost its immortality, the Halaku became the bringers of true death, and many shaped the realm of the restless dead.",
  "wod.demon.defilers": "The Defilers are the Fifth House of the Fallen, whose angelic name is Lammasu. Once they inspired humanity through the beauty of the seas and the stirring of the heart; after the Fall they became tempters who play on longing and desire. Their Lores are Longing, Storms and Transfiguration, letting them inflame emotions, command water and weather, and change their appearance. They are often called muses, sometimes for good and sometimes for ill.",
  "wod.demon.devils": "The Devils are the First House of the Fallen, the Namaru or Heralds, the house to which Lucifer belonged. They were God's messengers and leaders of the Host, and in the rebellion they became its commanders. Proud, charismatic and used to leading, Devils wield the Lores of the Celestials, Flame and Radiance. They draw others to them through sheer presence and conviction.",
  "wod.demon.devourers": "The Devourers are the Sixth House of the Fallen, whose angelic name is Rabisu. They were the shepherds of plants and animals, and they carry the primal vitality and hunger of living things. Their Lores are the Beast, the Wild and Flesh, which let them command animals, rouse nature and reshape living bodies. Devourers are wild, passionate and driven by instinct.",
  "wod.demon.fiends": "The Fiends are the Fourth House of the Fallen, whose angelic name is Neberu. They were the angels who set the stars in motion and read the pattern of creation. Their Lores are Light, Patterns and Portals, granting illusions, glimpses of fate and the power to open passages across space. Fiends are cool, analytical schemers who see the strings behind events.",
  "wod.demon.malefactors": "The Malefactors are the Third House of the Fallen, whose angelic name is Annunaki. They were the artificers who shaped the earth and made the tools of creation. Their Lores are the Earth, Paths and the Forge, granting power over stone and soil, over movement from place to place, and over the crafting of enchanted objects. Malefactors are practical, patient makers who value lasting works.",
  "wod.demon.scourges": "The Scourges are the Second House of the Fallen, whose angelic name is Asharu. They once breathed life into creatures and guarded humanity as healers and protectors. Their Lores are Awakening, the Firmament and the Winds, letting them heal or harm the body, sense far-off events and command the air. Many Scourges still feel a protective bond with mortals, while others turned that power into plague.",
  "wod.demon.slayers": "The Slayers are the Seventh House of the Fallen, whose angelic name is Halaku, the Angels of Death. They ended things whose time was over and guided souls onward. Their Lores are Death, the Spirit and the Realms, granting power over decay, over the souls of the dead and over passage between worlds. Slayers tend to be solemn and quiet, familiar with endings in a way no other house is.",
  "wod.fac.cryptic": "The Cryptics are a faction of the Fallen devoted to understanding. They want to know the truth behind God's plan, the reasons for the war and the Fall, and perhaps the meaning of existence itself. Cryptics investigate, gather lore and pursue mysteries, believing the answers will decide what the Fallen should do. They form a small and loosely connected faction.",
  "wod.fac.faustian": "The Faustians are a faction of the Fallen who see humanity as the key to power. They seek to bind mortals through pacts and to harvest their Faith, building strength to renew the war against Heaven. They are pragmatic, ambitious and often ruthless in their dealings with humans. Their name recalls the legend of Faust and his bargain.",
  "wod.fac.luciferan": "The Luciferans are a faction of the Fallen loyal to the memory of Lucifer, the Morningstar who led the rebellion. They believe he still exists somewhere and await his return so that he can lead them again. Until then they try to rebuild the old hierarchy and discipline of the rebel armies. They are the most organized and militant of the factions.",
  "wod.fac.ravener": "The Raveners are a faction of the Fallen who have given up hope of victory or forgiveness. Convinced that creation is beyond saving, they seek to destroy as much of it as possible as a final act of defiance. They are nihilistic and violent, and other demons regard them with fear and loathing. Many have grown close to the Earthbound and their designs.",
  "wod.fac.reconciler": "The Reconcilers are a faction of the Fallen who hope to undo the damage of the war. They want to restore the world to the Paradise it was meant to be and, perhaps, to earn forgiveness from God. Many work to heal both the land and humanity, and some seek a reconciliation between the Fallen and mortals. Their path is idealistic but hard, because the Torment of the Abyss still pulls at them.",
  "wod.faith": "Faith is the measure of a demon's power and its link to the divine spark of creation. Since their release from the Abyss, the Fallen must draw Faith from mortal belief, gathered through worship, pacts with thralls and acts that inspire devotion. Faith fuels the evocations of their Lores and allows them to reveal their apocalyptic form. A demon without Faith is little more than a voice in a stolen body.",
  "wod.fey.boggan": "Boggans are hardworking household fae, short and sturdy with clever hands. They take real pleasure in honest labor such as cooking, cleaning and craftwork, and they always seem to know the latest local gossip. Boggans gladly help the humble and those in need but have little patience for the arrogant and idle. Their weakness is a compulsion to finish tasks they begin and to aid those who truly need help.",
  "wod.fey.clurichaun": "Clurichauns are kin to the leprechauns of Irish folklore: jovial brawlers fond of drink, song and a good fight. They are charming, lucky and generous with friends but quick to take offense. Their legendary luck and knack for sniffing out hidden treasure serve them well. Their weakness is a fiery temper that makes it hard to back down from an insult or a quarrel.",
  "wod.fey.eshu": "The Eshu are wandering fae whose roots lie in the myths of Africa, India and the Near East. They travel endlessly in search of stories and adventure, and their gift guides them to wherever something important is happening. Eshu are superb storytellers who hold hospitality sacred. Their weakness is recklessness: they can hardly refuse a dare or a risky challenge.",
  "wod.fey.ghille": "The Ghille Dhu are fae of forests and growing things, kin to the spirits of trees and wild places. Their bodies take on the look of leaves, bark and vines, and they change with the turning of the seasons. They are guardians of the wild who feel deep sympathy for plants and nature. Cities and concrete weigh heavily on them, and most avoid the urban world.",
  "wod.fey.nocker": "Nockers are fae craftsmen and inventors with a genius for building the impossible out of Glamour and scrap. They are perfectionists who curse constantly, inventively and loudly, especially at their own work. Nockers can repair and create wonders that no mortal engineer could understand. Their weakness is that everything they build carries at least one hidden flaw.",
  "wod.fey.pooka": "Pooka are playful shapeshifting fae, each tied to a particular animal such as a cat, fox, raven or rabbit. They can take that animal's form and share many of its habits. Pooka are incorrigible pranksters, and their defining weakness is that they almost never tell the plain truth: they lie, embellish or answer in riddles. Friends learn that real warmth often hides behind their tall tales.",
  "wod.fey.redcap": "Redcaps are the most feared of the commoner kith, fae of appetite and menace named after the caps of folklore said to be dyed in blood. They can bite through and swallow almost anything, from wood to metal. Redcaps relish intimidation and dark humor, and other fae avoid crossing them. Their weakness is their sheer unpleasantness: people and animals alike instinctively distrust them.",
  "wod.fey.satyr": "Satyrs are fae of revelry and wild passion, drawn from the myths of ancient Greece, with goat legs and horns. They love music, wine, dance and lively philosophical debate, and their songs and dances can stir others to passion and push back Banality for a while. Satyrs are swift runners who distrust restraint and dull routine. Their weakness is that same passion: their moods swing wildly and they find it hard to resist temptation.",
  "wod.fey.selkie": "Selkies are fae of the sea from the folklore of the northern coasts, able to become seals with the help of their sealskin coats. They feel a deep bond with the ocean and its creatures and often act as protectors of marine life. A selkie's coat is precious: if it is lost or stolen, the selkie cannot change shape and is badly weakened. Many feel restless and homesick when far from the shore.",
  "wod.fey.sidhe": "The Sidhe are the faerie nobility, beautiful and imperious beings who returned from Arcadia in 1969 and claimed rule over the other kith. They are grouped into noble houses and lead most freeholds and kingdoms. Their unearthly grace makes them hard to ignore and harder to refuse. Their weakness is fragility before Banality, which hits them harder than it does the commoner fae.",
  "wod.fey.sluagh": "The Sluagh are pale, quiet fae of shadows, secrets and dusty forgotten places. They are expert gatherers of information and listen far more than they speak, often keeping company with ghosts. Sluagh can squeeze through tight spaces and notice what others miss. Their weakness is that they cannot raise their voices above a whisper.",
  "wod.fey.troll": "Trolls are huge, strong and steadfast fae, famed as loyal guardians and warriors. They take vows and duty extremely seriously, and their word is their bond. Trolls are often quiet and patient but terrible when roused to defend those they protect. Their weakness is that breaking a sworn oath or abandoning a duty robs them of their great strength.",
  "wod.glamour": "Glamour is the essence of dreams and creativity, the lifeblood of the fae and of the Dreaming itself. Changelings spend it to work cantrips, to reveal the true faerie nature of things and to sustain chimerical creations. They gain it from inspiration and art, from shared Reverie with mortals and from treasured places called freeholds. A changeling starved of Glamour drifts toward Banality and forgetfulness.",
  "wod.house.aesin": "House Aesin is a noble house descended from the Norse fae, who claim kinship with the Aesir and Vanir of myth. Mostly Unseelie, they spent long ages in the icy realm of Alfheim before returning to the mortal world. Their society is hierarchical and martial, guided by Nine Virtues such as courage, honor, hospitality and self-reliance. Members of the house can speak with forest animals, but they find it hard to treat commoners as equals.",
  "wod.house.ailil": "House Ailil is the leading house of the Unseelie Court, famed for politics, intrigue and long-term scheming. Its members prefer subtlety and manipulation to open confrontation, and many are active in the Shadow Court. They are skilled diplomats who read situations quickly and exploit every opening. Their weakness is pride in their own cunning, which makes it very hard for them to admit a mistake.",
  "wod.house.balor": "House Balor is an Unseelie house that traces its blood to the Fomorians and to Balor of the Evil Eye. Organized like an army, it sees itself in perpetual war against the Seelie order and prepares for an Endless Winter in which it will rule. Every member bears some deformity of body or spirit, treated as a challenge to overcome. Unlike other sidhe, they can bear contact with cold iron without being hindered.",
  "wod.house.beaumayn": "House Beaumayn is a Seelie house of prophets, founded by a prophet-knight in the Mythic Age. All of its members receive glimpses of possible futures in dreams and meditation. Imprisoned in Arcadia after a past scandal and wiped from memory, the house returned only recently and is still viewed with suspicion. Its visions also draw the attention of the Shadow Court and the Thallain.",
  "wod.house.daireann": "House Daireann is an old house of Celtic warriors, healers and champions who recently emerged from Arcadia. Loosely aligned with the Unseelie Court, it blends traditions from both courts and follows the Three Laws of honor, vengeance and hospitality. Its warriors fight with a near-berserk fury tempered by strict honor and rarely flee a battle. Their weakness is a love of boasting that can loosen their tongues and spill secrets.",
  "wod.house.dougal": "House Dougal is a Seelie house of master smiths and craftspeople, the unseen center of the sidhe that supplies weapons, armor and inventions to other houses. Status within the house comes from skill and deeds rather than politics. Many members have a physical impairment, often offset by devices of their own making. Fine engineering and well-made machines can captivate them for hours.",
  "wod.house.eiluned": "House Eiluned is a house of sorcerers, scholars and keepers of secrets, nominally Seelie but at home in moral gray areas. Its members value knowledge as power and often serve as advisors and strategists rather than rulers. Their cantrips are unusually potent. Other houses distrust them for their secrecy, and their curiosity constantly draws them into conspiracies.",
  "wod.house.fiona": "House Fiona is a Seelie house of passionate romantics and fearless adventurers. Its members throw themselves wholeheartedly into love, quests and battle, and they are famed for their courage. They are warm, generous and loyal to their friends. Their weakness is that same passion: they fall in love easily and recklessly, often with unsuitable partners, and rarely back down from danger.",
  "wod.house.gwydion": "House Gwydion is one of the most powerful Seelie houses and the house of the High King of Concordia. Its members see themselves as born leaders, devoted to justice, honor and the defense of their realms. They are perceptive judges who are hard to deceive. Their weakness is a fierce temper and a suspicious nature that can turn into wrath when their pride is wounded.",
  "wod.house.leanhaun": "House Leanhaun is an Unseelie house of artists, patrons and muses who cultivate mortal Dreamers and draw out extraordinary creative work. They are charismatic and graceful, and they excel as entertainers, talent scouts and spies. Behind the elegance lies a curse: they must keep harvesting Glamour from those they inspire, or they begin to wither rapidly. Many secretly support the Shadow Court.",
  "wod.house.liam": "House Liam is an exiled Seelie house whose founder, Lord Liam, was banished for eloquently defending mortals. Its members are sages, lore-keepers and storytellers who quietly protect humans from Banality and from fae predation. They find it easy to touch mortals with Glamour. Other fae call them oathbreakers and often refuse to honor their oaths or offer them hospitality.",
  "wod.house.scathach": "House Scathach was founded by Scathach, the legendary warrior-woman of Irish myth. It stands apart from both courts, rejects hereditary rule and values service, travel and martial skill. Its members, sometimes called Gray Walkers, are reserved, silent in battle and more diverse in background than other sidhe. Unique among the noble houses, they went through the Changeling Way before the Shattering, and other nobles often regard them coolly.",
  "wod.house.varich": "House Varich is an Unseelie house from Russia, descended from the ancient fae once worshipped there, with roots in medieval Novgorod. It is feudal and strictly hierarchical, valuing loyalty, strategy and fine work in amber and gold. Members can learn to anticipate patterns in fights or dances by watching them. They cannot swear oaths of love and must accept every challenge offered to them.",
  "wod.lore.awakening": "The Lore of Awakening is one of the Lores of the Scourges. In the age of creation it allowed the Asharu to breathe the spark of life into new bodies and to shield their charges from sickness and injury. In the modern world it lets a demon heal wounds, cure illness and even restore life to the recently dead. At high Torment the same knowledge can inflict disease and decay.",
  "wod.lore.beasts": "The Lore of the Beast is one of the Lores of the Devourers. It grants mastery over animals, fish and birds, letting a demon summon them, command them and even reshape their bodies. The Rabisu once used it to shepherd every creature of the wild. It also lets a demon touch the primal instincts that humans share with beasts.",
  "wod.lore.celestials": "The Lore of the Celestials is one of the Lores of the Devils. In Heaven it let the Namaru locate, inform and support other angels; now it draws on mortal Faith. A demon who knows it can sense the presence of other spiritual beings, speak across distances and invoke the power of the divine names. It is a lore of communication, authority and holy power.",
  "wod.lore.death": "The Lore of Death is one of the Lores of the Slayers. Originally it allowed the Halaku to end life swiftly and painlessly and to break dead matter down so creation could begin anew. In demon hands it hastens decay, withers living things and inflicts death. It reflects the house's duty as the ender of what has run its course.",
  "wod.lore.earth": "The Lore of the Earth is one of the Lores of the Malefactors. It holds the secrets of shaping rock and soil, from moving a single stone to reshaping the landscape. The Annunaki once used it to form and maintain the face of Paradise. A demon with this lore can pass through stone, raise walls of earth or shake the ground itself.",
  "wod.lore.firmament": "The Lore of the Firmament is one of the Lores of the Scourges. It restores a part of the far-reaching awareness the Asharu once had, letting them sense when those in their care are suffering or in danger. A demon who knows it can perceive distant places and events and reach out across great distances. It is a lore of watchfulness and protection.",
  "wod.lore.flame": "The Lore of Flame is one of the Lores of the Devils. It grants mastery over the primal, purifying fire of creation, from kindling a candle to calling down a blaze. During the war it became one of the Namaru's foremost weapons. A demon who knows it can summon, control and shape fire, and shield against it.",
  "wod.lore.flesh": "The Lore of Flesh is one of the Lores of the Devourers. It developed during the war against Heaven, when the Rabisu learned to reshape living bodies for battle. With it a demon can sculpt flesh, grow natural weapons, heal or twist living tissue, and strengthen the body. It is one of the most visceral and feared Lores.",
  "wod.lore.forge": "The Lore of the Forge is one of the Lores of the Malefactors. It is the practical side of their creative power, the knowledge of crafting tools, weapons and objects of wondrous quality. A demon with this lore can repair or destroy objects and enchant items with lasting power. Many of the most dangerous artifacts of the war were made with it.",
  "wod.lore.light": "The Lore of Light is one of the Lores of the Fiends. It covers the nature of light and perception, and the Neberu learned to bend it into powerful illusions. A demon who knows it can cloak itself in shadow, dazzle enemies and weave convincing images that deceive the senses. It became a key weapon in the Fiends' arsenal of misdirection.",
  "wod.lore.longing": "The Lore of Longing is one of the Lores of the Defilers, the core wisdom of their house. It grants the power to stir the human heart, awakening desire, hope, sorrow or inspiration. The Lammasu once used it to lift humanity toward truth and beauty. After the Fall it is just as often used to tempt and manipulate.",
  "wod.lore.paths": "The Lore of Paths is one of the Lores of the Malefactors. It governs how a physical object travels from one point to another, the roads and routes of the material world. A demon who knows it can always find the way, seal or open passages, and speed or hinder movement. It is the quiet art of the journey.",
  "wod.lore.patterns": "The Lore of Patterns is one of the Lores of the Fiends. It lets a demon study the motions of the Grand Design and foresee problems before they occur. With it the Neberu can read probabilities, glimpse what may come and subtly nudge chance and fate. It is the lore of prophecy and destiny.",
  "wod.lore.portals": "The Lore of Portals is one of the Lores of the Fiends. It holds the secrets of crossing reality in an instant, opening doorways between distant places. A demon who knows it can lock and unlock passages, sense ways through space and travel without crossing the distance between. In creation it let the Neberu move freely through the cosmos they tended.",
  "wod.lore.radiance": "The Lore of Radiance is one of the Lores of the Devils. It holds the secrets of inspiration and leadership, and in the Age of Wrath the Namaru developed it to stir mortal allies. A demon who knows it can project an aura of majesty, rally followers, inspire awe or terror and command attention. It is the lore of presence and leadership.",
  "wod.lore.realms": "The Lore of the Realms is one of the Lores of the Slayers. It deals with the boundaries between the physical world and the pockets of reality beyond it. The Halaku used it to stabilize and travel to realms outside the cosmos, and it laid a foundation for the Shadowlands of the dead. A demon who knows it can sense and cross the borders between worlds.",
  "wod.lore.spirit": "The Lore of the Spirit is one of the Lores of the Slayers. It concerns the souls of the dead, and with it the Halaku could anchor spirits to places on Earth so they would not be lost. A demon who knows it can see and speak with ghosts, command them or bind them. It is the lore of the restless dead.",
  "wod.lore.storms": "The Lore of Storms is one of the Lores of the Defilers. It grants mastery over wind and wave, from calming waters to raising tempests. With it the Lammasu extended their influence from the seas over dry land. A demon who knows it can call rain, command water and unleash the fury of the weather.",
  "wod.lore.transfiguration": "The Lore of Transfiguration is one of the Lores of the Defilers. It lets a demon change its appearance at will to match what those around it expect or desire. It covers masks, disguises and the reshaping of one's own form. In the hands of the Lammasu it is a lore of seduction and deception as much as beauty.",
  "wod.lore.wild": "The Lore of the Wild is one of the Lores of the Devourers. It gathers the secrets of forests and fields, letting a demon command plants and shape natural environments. The Rabisu once tended the growing things of the world with it. A demon who knows it can make plants grow, wither or move and turn the wilderness itself against intruders.",
  "wod.lore.winds": "The Lore of the Winds is one of the Lores of the Scourges. It governs the currents of air that sustain life, from a gentle breath to a howling gale. The Asharu once used it to spread the breath of life through the world. A demon who knows it can command the wind, carry voices and scents, and protect or deny the very air others breathe.",
  "wod.seem.grump": "Grump is the seeming of an older changeling, the last stage of a mortal life lived alongside a fae soul. Grumps are closer to Banality and have less Glamour, but they carry long experience, deep knowledge of faerie lore and a sharp tongue. They are often keepers of tradition and teachers of cantrips, known for complaining yet valued for their wisdom. Many younger changelings learn to look past the gruffness to the care behind it.",
  "wod.seem.wilder": "Wilder is the seeming of a young adult changeling whose fae soul still burns brightly. Wilders are full of energy, passion and rebellion, eager to experience everything and to test themselves against the world. They are searching for their own path and torn between their Seelie and Unseelie sides. Wilders make up most adventuring changelings, though their impetuousness often leads to trouble.",
  "leg.l.al": "The XX Legion, led by the Primarch Alpharius, the last of the Primarchs to be found. The Alpha Legion fought through infiltration, misdirection and layered deception, with agents and cells hidden among their enemies, and took the many-headed hydra as their emblem. They sided with Horus in the Heresy, though their true motives remain famously unclear.",
  "leg.l.ba": "The IX Legion, led by the winged Primarch Sanguinius, whose sons were recruited from the radiation-scarred world of Baal. The Blood Angels are noble warriors and fine artists, but carry a hidden curse in their gene-seed: the Red Thirst and the Black Rage. They stayed loyal, and Sanguinius fell fighting Horus aboard the Warmaster's flagship during the Siege of Terra.",
  "leg.l.da": "The I Legion, the first created and led by the Primarch Lion El'Jonson of the forest world Caliban. The Dark Angels were masters of every form of warfare and kept many secret weapons and orders. They remained loyal, but part of the Legion turned against the Lion, and Caliban was destroyed; the hunt for these Fallen has been their hidden shame ever since.",
  "leg.l.dg": "The XIV Legion, led by the Primarch Mortarion, raised on the poison-choked world of Barbarus. The Death Guard prized grim endurance, resistance to toxins and relentless advance. They joined Horus, and while stranded in the Warp they were struck by a terrible plague, becoming the bloated, unkillable Plague Marines of Nurgle.",
  "leg.l.ec": "The III Legion, led by the Primarch Fulgrim and rebuilt from near-extinction on the world of Chemos. The Emperor's Children were obsessed with perfection and were honoured with the right to bear the Emperor's own aquila. Their pride led them to the Chaos god Slaanesh, and as traitors they abandoned every limit in pursuit of excess and sensation.",
  "leg.l.if": "The VII Legion, led by the Primarch Rogal Dorn, famed as the finest builders and defenders of fortifications. The Imperial Fists were steadfast and unbending, and their fortress-ship the Phalanx was their home. Remaining loyal, they fortified the Imperial Palace and held it against the traitors during the Siege of Terra.",
  "leg.l.ih": "The X Legion, led by the Primarch Ferrus Manus of the harsh world Medusa. The Iron Hands believe that \"the flesh is weak\" and replace their bodies with bionics, valuing cold logic over emotion. Loyal to the Emperor, they lost their Primarch at the Dropsite Massacre on Isstvan V, where Ferrus Manus was slain by Fulgrim.",
  "leg.l.iw": "The IV Legion, led by the Primarch Perturabo of Olympia, the masters of siegecraft and attrition. The Iron Warriors were endlessly given the grinding, thankless sieges of the Great Crusade, and their bitterness grew with every campaign. They joined Horus, and their motto is \"Iron within, iron without\".",
  "leg.l.nl": "The VIII Legion, led by the Primarch Konrad Curze, the Night Haunter, of the lightless world Nostramo. The Night Lords waged war through terror, breaking whole worlds by fear before and during battle. They turned traitor, and Curze, haunted by visions of his own death, allowed an Imperial assassin to kill him.",
  "leg.l.rg": "The XIX Legion, led by the Primarch Corvus Corax, who freed the prison moon of Deliverance. The Raven Guard specialise in stealth, speed and precise strikes from the shadows. Loyal to the Emperor, they were nearly destroyed at the Dropsite Massacre on Isstvan V but endured and rebuilt.",
  "leg.l.sal": "The XVIII Legion, led by the Primarch Vulkan of the volcanic world Nocturne. The Salamanders are master smiths who forge their own weapons, favour flamers and melta weapons, and are known for protecting ordinary people. They stayed loyal, suffering terribly at Isstvan V.",
  "leg.l.soh": "The XVI Legion, originally the Luna Wolves, renamed in honour of their Primarch Horus Lupercal when he became Warmaster. The Sons of Horus were the Emperor's foremost Legion until Horus turned to Chaos and led the rebellion that became the Horus Heresy. After his death, the Legion was renamed the Black Legion under Abaddon.",
  "leg.l.sw": "The VI Legion, led by the Primarch Leman Russ of the ice world Fenris. The Space Wolves are fierce, savage warriors with their own traditions and Rune Priests, and served as the Emperor's executioners. Remaining loyal, they were sent against Magnus the Red, and their assault destroyed Prospero, homeworld of the Thousand Sons.",
  "leg.l.ts": "The XV Legion, led by the psychic Primarch Magnus the Red of Prospero, the most learned scholars and sorcerers among the Legions. The Thousand Sons were plagued by mutation, and their reliance on psychic power made them suspect. After Prospero was burned, they joined Horus and Tzeentch; the sorcerer Ahriman's Rubric later turned most of them into dust inside sealed armour.",
  "leg.l.um": "The XIII Legion, led by the Primarch Roboute Guilliman, ruler of Macragge and the realm of Ultramar. The Ultramarines were the largest Legion, renowned for discipline, logistics and order. They stayed loyal, and after the Heresy Guilliman wrote the Codex Astartes, which split the Legions into Chapters and still guides most Space Marines.",
  "leg.l.wb": "The XVII Legion, led by the Primarch Lorgar of Colchis, who worshipped the Emperor as a god. After the Emperor rebuked them at Monarchia, the Word Bearers turned to the Chaos gods in secret. They were the first Legion to fall to Chaos and helped draw Horus into the rebellion.",
  "leg.l.we": "The XII Legion, once the War Hounds, led by the Primarch Angron, a former gladiator. Angron forced his warriors to take the Butcher's Nails, implants that flood the brain with rage, and the World Eaters became the most savage of the Legions. They joined Horus and gave themselves entirely to Khorne.",
  "leg.l.ws": "The V Legion, led by the Primarch Jaghatai Khan of the steppe world Chogoris. The White Scars are masters of lightning warfare: swift strikes, mounted assaults and relentless pursuit. They remained loyal, fought their way back to Terra and helped defend the Imperial Palace during the Siege.",
  "leg.lost": "The Second and Eleventh Legions are missing from every Imperial record. Something happened to them and their Primarchs during the Great Crusade, and their names and histories were deliberately erased. Those who know the truth are bound to silence, and nothing reliable about their fate is known.",
  "ow.grudges": "The Dammaz Kron, the Great Book of Grudges, is kept by the High King of the Dwarfs in Karaz-a-Karak and records every wrong ever done to the Dwarf people. By tradition, entries are written in the blood of the High King. A grudge is struck out only once it has been avenged, and every hold, clan and family keeps its own book as well.",
  "ow.r.beast": "The Beastmen are horned, goat-headed children of Chaos who lurk in the deep forests of the Old World. They gather in herds around sacred herdstones and hate civilisation, raiding villages and towns to tear down what humans have built. Gors, Ungors, Minotaurs and monsters fight together under a beastlord.",
  "ow.r.bret": "Bretonnia is a kingdom of knights, ruled by a king and devoted to the Lady of the Lake. Its nobility lives by a code of chivalry, while peasants serve below them. The greatest knights quest for the Grail; those who succeed become Grail Knights, and the kingdom's power rests on charges of heavy cavalry.",
  "ow.r.cathay": "Grand Cathay is a vast empire in the far east, ruled by the Dragon Emperor and his children, ancient dragons who can take human form. Its armies guard the Great Bastion, an enormous wall holding back the forces of Chaos from the north. Cathay values harmony and order, and its goods reach the Old World along the Ivory Road.",
  "ow.r.cd": "The Chaos Dwarfs are descendants of Dwarfs who were cut off in the Dark Lands and turned to worship Hashut, the bull-headed Father of Darkness. From their great ziggurat city they rule an industrial realm built on slave labour, sorcery and daemon-bound war machines. Their sorcerers wield great power but slowly turn to stone.",
  "ow.r.de": "The Dark Elves are a cruel, exiled people from the cold land of Naggaroth, ruled by the Witch King Malekith. They worship Khaine, the god of murder, and launch raids across the world from their black arks to take captives and treasure. Their hatred for their kin, the High Elves, is unending.",
  "ow.r.dwarf": "The Dwarfs are a proud, stubborn people of ancient mountain holds, the greatest of which is Karaz-a-Karak. They are superb miners, smiths and engineers, honour their ancestor gods, and their Runesmiths bind magic into runes. They never forget a wrong, and their empire, though diminished, fights on against orcs, goblins and skaven.",
  "ow.r.emp": "The Empire is the largest and most powerful human realm of the Old World, founded by Sigmar, who is now worshipped as a god. It is ruled by an Emperor chosen by the Elector Counts of its provinces. Its armies combine state troops, knights, battle wizards from the Colleges of Magic, and gunpowder engines and steam tanks.",
  "ow.r.he": "The High Elves are an ancient, proud people from the island of Ulthuan, ruled by the Phoenix King and the Everqueen. They are masters of magic and the sea, and they maintain the Great Vortex that drains away the raw power of Chaos. Their disciplined armies of spearmen, archers and elite warriors fight to protect the world against Chaos.",
  "ow.r.kislev": "Kislev is a northern human realm of steppes and forests that stands between the Old World and the Chaos Wastes. It is ruled by a Tzar or Tzarina, and its rulers have often wielded powerful ice magic. Kislevites are hardy and fiercely independent, and their armies of winged lancers, horse archers and stubborn infantry hold the line against invasions from the north.",
  "ow.r.liz": "The Lizardmen are the servants of the Old Ones, dwelling in the steaming jungles and temple-cities of Lustria. They are led by the slann, ancient mage-priests of immense power, and fight with saurus warriors, skinks and great reptilian beasts. Their purpose is to carry out the Great Plan of their vanished creators.",
  "ow.r.ogre": "The Ogres are huge, ever-hungry brutes from the Mountains of Mourn who worship the Great Maw, a god of endless hunger. Their tribes are ruled by the strongest Tyrant, and anything can end up in their bellies. Many travel the world as mercenaries, valued for their strength and their willingness to fight for pay or food.",
  "ow.r.orc": "Orcs & Goblins are the greenskins, a tough and numerous race that lives for fighting. They worship Gork and Mork, and when a strong warboss rises, tribes gather into a great Waaagh! that sweeps across the land. Big brutal orcs and sneaky goblins, with trolls and giants, form vast, unruly hordes.",
  "ow.r.skaven": "The Skaven are rat-men who live in a vast Under-Empire tunnelled beneath the world, with their capital at Skavenblight. They worship the Horned Rat and are ruled by the Council of Thirteen, while rival clans scheme against each other. Using warpstone, plague and strange machines, they plan to overrun the surface world, though most humans think they are a myth.",
  "ow.r.tk": "The Tomb Kings are the ancient rulers of Nehekhara, a desert land of great pyramids, raised from death by Nagash's terrible ritual. Bound to their bodies, they command legions of skeletal warriors and animated constructs, with Liche Priests performing the rites. They seek to restore the lost glory of their kingdoms.",
  "ow.r.vamp": "The Vampires are immortal nobles who rule over the dead, most infamously in the cursed land of Sylvania. Using necromancy, they raise armies of zombies, skeletons and ghouls to serve them. Their bloodlines, such as the von Carsteins, have repeatedly threatened the Empire.",
  "ow.r.we": "The Wood Elves are the guardians of Athel Loren, an enchanted forest in the Old World. They live in harmony with its spirits and serve the forest's rulers, Orion and Ariel. They are unmatched archers and strike swiftly from the trees, defending their realm from any intruder.",
  "ow.r.woc": "The Warriors of Chaos are mortal champions from the frozen north who serve the Chaos gods in hope of glory and favour. Clad in heavy armour and often bearing gifts of mutation, they lead great invasions south against the civilised lands. The most favoured may rise to daemonhood; those who fail may become mindless spawn.",
  "wh.f.aeldari": "The Aeldari are an ancient, psychically gifted race whose great empire collapsed in the Fall, when their own excesses gave birth to the Chaos god Slaanesh. The survivors live on vast craftworlds and follow strict Paths to control their nature. They fight with elegant technology and powerful psykers, and preserve their souls in spirit stones.",
  "wh.f.csm": "The Chaos Space Marines are renegade Space Marines who have turned against the Imperium and serve the Ruinous Powers. Many are veterans of the Traitor Legions who fled into the Eye of Terror after the Horus Heresy and have waged the Long War ever since. They fight with ancient wargear, daemons and mutations granted by their gods.",
  "wh.f.dmech": "The Dark Mechanicum are the tech-priests of Mars who sided with Horus and embraced Chaos. They pursue forbidden knowledge and technology without restraint, binding daemons into machines and creating daemon engines. Their creations serve the traitor forces to this day.",
  "wh.f.drukhari": "The Drukhari are the cruel kin of the Aeldari who dwell in Commorragh, a dark city hidden in the webway. To stave off Slaanesh's claim on their souls, they sustain themselves by inflicting suffering on others. They launch swift raids into realspace to take captives, using fast skimmers and deadly blades.",
  "wh.f.gk": "The Grey Knights are a secret Chapter of Space Marines, made up entirely of psykers and devoted to fighting daemons. They serve the Ordo Malleus of the Inquisition from their fortress on Titan. Armed with Nemesis force weapons and protected by blessed armour, they are among the most incorruptible warriors of the Imperium.",
  "wh.f.harlequins": "The Harlequins are mysterious Aeldari warrior-performers devoted to Cegorach, the Laughing God. They travel through the webway in troupes, acting out sacred myths and joining battles with acrobatic grace. They are also the guardians of the Black Library, a hidden repository of forbidden knowledge.",
  "wh.f.ig": "The Imperial Guard, also called the Astra Militarum, is the vast army of the Imperium, made of countless regiments raised from worlds across the galaxy. Its soldiers are ordinary humans armed with lasguns, supported by tanks and artillery. What they lack in strength they make up for with numbers, courage and endless sacrifice.",
  "wh.f.mech": "The Adeptus Mechanicus is the priesthood of Mars, worshipping the Machine God, also called the Omnissiah. Its tech-priests preserve and maintain the Imperium's technology, guarding ancient knowledge and replacing their flesh with machinery. It fields armies of skitarii, servitors and war engines.",
  "wh.f.necrons": "The Necrons are an ancient race whose living bodies were transferred into undying metal forms in a pact with the star-gods called the C'tan. After sleeping for millions of years in their tomb worlds, they are awakening to reclaim the galaxy. They fight with gauss weapons and terrifying technology, and their bodies repair themselves.",
  "wh.f.orks": "The Orks are a green-skinned race that lives for war and is spread across the galaxy. They are tough and resilient, and their crude technology often works only because they believe it does. When enough Orks gather under a strong warboss, they launch a WAAAGH!",
  "wh.f.sm": "The Space Marines, also called the Adeptus Astartes, are genetically enhanced warriors of the Imperium. Organised into Chapters of about a thousand warriors, they are the Emperor's Angels of Death, deployed to the most dangerous battlefields. Clad in power armour and armed with bolters, they are the Imperium's finest soldiers.",
  "wh.f.sob": "The Sisters of Battle, also known as the Adepta Sororitas, are the military orders of the Ecclesiarchy, the Imperial church. They fight with unshakeable faith in the Emperor, armed with bolters, flamers and meltas. Their faith is so strong that it sometimes manifests as miracles on the battlefield.",
  "wh.f.tau": "The T'au are a young, technologically advanced race from the Eastern Fringe of the galaxy, united by the philosophy of the Greater Good. Their society is divided into castes and led by the mysterious Ethereals. Their armies rely on battlesuits and powerful pulse weapons, and they often seek to incorporate other races peacefully.",
  "wh.f.tyranids": "The Tyranids are an extragalactic swarm guided by a single Hive Mind. Their hive fleets travel between stars, consuming all biological matter on the worlds they invade to create new creatures. They adapt rapidly to any defence and have devoured countless worlds.",
  "wh.waaagh": "A WAAAGH! is both a great Ork crusade and the psychic power generated by Orks in large numbers. When a warboss gathers enough followers, the WAAAGH! grows louder and stronger, drawing in more Orks. The greater the WAAAGH!, the more powerful the Orks become.",
  "wr.mut": "Mutations are the gifts of the Chaos gods, marks of favour that warp the body of their followers. They can range from extra eyes or limbs to fangs, horns or tentacles. For a champion of Chaos, mutations are signs of favour that may lead to daemonhood, but too many can turn them into a mindless spawn.",
  "wr.of.khorne": "Khorne is the Blood God, the Chaos god of war, rage and martial strength, who sits upon a throne of skulls. To serve Khorne is to fight endlessly, taking skulls in his name and despising sorcery and cowardice. His followers believe there is honour in combat.",
  "wr.of.nurgle": "Nurgle is the Plague God, also called Grandfather Nurgle, the Chaos god of disease and decay, but also of endurance and rebirth. His followers are blessed with diseases that make them immune to pain and despair. To serve Nurgle is to embrace the cycle of life and death.",
  "wr.of.rat": "The Horned Rat is the god of the Skaven, sometimes worshipped as a fifth Chaos god. His followers are scheming, ambitious and treacherous, always looking for ways to betray others. To serve the Horned Rat is to plot for the Skaven to rule the world.",
  "wr.of.slaanesh": "Slaanesh is the Dark Prince, the Chaos god of excess, perfection and sensation. His followers seek perfection in all things and push themselves ever further in pursuit of new experiences. To serve Slaanesh is to never be satisfied.",
  "wr.of.tzeentch": "Tzeentch is the Changer of Ways, the Chaos god of change, ambition and sorcery. His followers are scheming sorcerers who seek knowledge and power. To serve Tzeentch is to become a part of his endless plans.",
  "rz.s.pride": "Pride is the sin of those who stand above everyone else. In Re:Zero its Witch Factor grants an Authority that turns guilt into a weapon: whoever feels guilty before its bearer is broken apart by that very guilt. Who holds the Witch Factor of Pride today is unknown.",
  "rz.s.greed": "Greed is wanting everything and giving up nothing. Its Authorities stop time: the bearer can freeze the time of their own body and of everything they wear, and nothing can touch them. Small King ties this power to the hearts of others, and Cor Leonis lets the bearer take the burdens of their companions upon themselves while making them stronger.",
  "rz.s.envy": "Envy is longing for what others have. The power of its Witch is shadow: a darkness that reaches out, swallows and holds on. It is the most feared of the seven sins — the world still remembers its Witch as the one who swallowed half of it.",
  "rz.s.wrath": "Wrath is feeling everything at once and making others feel it too. Its Authority spreads the bearer's emotions to everyone around until they all share one feeling — pain included. Its bearer also commands chains and can turn them into fire.",
  "rz.s.sloth": "Sloth is the sin of doing nothing — and of the madness behind it. Its Authority sends out a black wave that clouds the minds of those nearby, and grants the Unseen Hand: invisible hands that do the work for their bearer, lifting, holding and crushing from afar.",
  "rz.s.lust": "Lust is the wish to be desired. Its Authority, Variation and Change, reshapes bodies: the bearer can take on any form, give others new ones, and even mend wounds by reshaping the flesh.",
  "rz.s.gluttony": "Gluttony is a hunger that is never satisfied. Its Authority devours a person's name and memories, so that the world forgets them. Its Eclipse lets the eater use the skills of those they have devoured, or take on their appearance.",
  "rz.r.follower": "A follower is one of the many members of the Witch Cult who serve a sin. A follower carries no Witch Factor: faith is their only power.",
  "rz.r.archbishop": "A Sin Archbishop is one of the leaders of the Witch Cult. Each Archbishop carries the Witch Factor of their sin and wields its Authority.",
  "rz.r.witch": "A Witch is the one in whom the sin first took shape. The Witches of the seven sins lived long ago; their Witch Factors outlived them and still pass from bearer to bearer.",
});
